window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-3ac2c00febcf3308"]={"key":"ny_c-3ac2c00febcf3308","group":"ny_c","uid":"3ac2c00febcf3308","rank":64,"title":"stonebridge refugee packet caseworker training","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c3","prompt":"You’re Selma Rutkowski, the refugee health nurse practitioner consulting to Stonebridge Board on the arrival-support contract delivered by Tanneryside Studios. Kamil Ekstrom needs the caseworker training package ready by 4:00 p.m. on August 23 so it can be approved for the September cohort and used in staff huddles before the first airport pickups. Tanneryside places about 300 arrivals a year across its Duluth, Minnesota, and Superior, Wisconsin, teams. Its caseworkers are not clinicians, but during the airport-to-apartment week they are normally the first local staff to open a family’s overseas medical examination packet, notice its classifications and vaccination record, and arrange the initial appointments. The interim briefing they use now is a verbal script that varies by supervisor, and Stonebridge Board has authorized you to replace it with a controlled training package effective September 3. The replacement needs to record that change clearly enough for Felix Dalgaard in contracts to identify which guidance is current without turning the material into a legal memorandum. The immediate problem is not that staff are unwilling to schedule care; it is that they are trying to be helpful from labels they only partly understand. Several staff have been using a rule of thumb that every notation beginning with “B” can wait for the ordinary new-arrival appointment, while others treat any pulmonary notation as proof that a person has active tuberculosis. That shortcut does not hold across the packets this program receives, but staff still need a practical way to decide what they may schedule themselves, what needs clinical or public-health coordination, and what is an emergency without drifting into diagnosis or triage. A recent B1 pulmonary notation on a child’s paperwork was repeated to a housing contact as “the child has TB,” which is exactly the kind of unsupported statement this package must prevent. Blank spaces in vaccination columns create a similar problem: some staff call them proof that no vaccine was given, while others ignore the vaccination page altogether. The training must let a nonclinical caseworker act confidently while keeping every simplification true to what the overseas record can and cannot establish. There is also a rare but consequential arrival pathway involving a Class A TB notation and a waiver. Kamil does not want that possibility buried inside routine guidance because staff may otherwise place the packet in the normal appointment queue, yet he also does not want a frightening presentation that causes caseworkers to isolate families, cancel transport, warn landlords, or make claims about infectiousness. The same restraint matters for mental-health, behavioral, sexually transmitted infection, and other Class A or B notations: the program needs operational guidance, not labels repeated beyond the people who need them. Caseworkers may encounter someone who is acutely ill or expressing an immediate risk of harm during arrival, but the packet itself is not a substitute for responding to what is happening in front of them. Tanneryside serves both sides of the Minnesota–Wisconsin border, and the family’s apartment, receiving clinic, and public-health handoff are not always in the same state as the airport pickup. Kamil wants the package to make the jurisdictional distinction usable without implying that the federal overseas classification changes meaning at the state line or inventing a state deadline, legal mandate, or contact detail that has not been approved. Selma remains the designated clinical escalation point for this contract, while emergency services remain the route for an immediate emergency. The local clinic and public-health contact directory is being renegotiated and is deliberately not part of this release, so use stable role-based directions rather than unverified names or numbers. Kamil and Felix have not settled whether the best treatment is a single common workflow with clearly marked Minnesota and Wisconsin branches or two visibly separate state lanes inside the same materials. Either approach can work, but make the choice deliberately and make it obvious to a day-one caseworker which route applies. Produce `caseworker_training_deck.pdf`, a caseworker-facing deck of no more than 10 slides, and `first_week_action_key.docx`, a one-page desk key that can be used while a packet is open. The deck should be suitable for a 20-minute huddle led by a nonclinical supervisor, and the key must still work when used on its own by a new caseworker who has never seen an overseas medical packet. Use plain, non-stigmatizing language, keep personal health information to the minimum needed for scheduling and escalation, and do not reproduce a realistic patient packet or include identifying case details. Any classification meanings, scheduling windows, escalation timing, or other figures appearing in both files must agree exactly. Include a concise effective-date or revision statement showing that this package replaces the interim verbal briefing, but do not add application screenshots, billing instructions, clinical treatment recommendations, or an exhaustive immigration-law explanation. There are no attachments; use your refugee-health practice knowledge and keep any examples synthetic. Write both files into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filenames given.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `caseworker_training_deck.pdf` — A caseworker-facing training deck for a 20-minute staff huddle on using overseas medical packet information during the first arrival week. (No more than 10 slides/pages; suitable for facilitation by a nonclinical supervisor; effective for Minnesota and Wisconsin operations; must agree with the action key on shared labels, figures, and timing.)\n- `first_week_action_key.docx` — A one-page standalone action key for a day-one caseworker using an overseas medical packet while arranging first-week services. (Exactly one rendered page; usable without the deck; must agree with the deck on shared labels, figures, and timing.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/caseworker_training_deck.pdf","bytes":18212,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/8a202e92d3baaa7f13008b41493f55e33c6980c5bfed7b74dc886d634b56db13.pdf","sha256":"8a202e92d3baaa7f13008b41493f55e33c6980c5bfed7b74dc886d634b56db13","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"First-week packet actions\nA 20-minute huddle for nonclinical caseworkers | Duluth, Minnesota and Superior, Wisconsin teams\n\n\nPurpose                                                                                                 Your role\nTurn overseas medical packet information into safe scheduling and escalation                            Observe, record, schedule, safeguard information, and escalate. You do not\nactions. Keep the record’s words intact; do not turn labels into diagnoses.                             diagnose, decide treatment, determine infectiousness, or conduct clinical\n                                                                                                        triage.\n\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                          1 / 10\n\f The priority order: respond before you read\n Use this sequence every time. It is action routing, not diagnosis.\n\nACT FIRST                   Caseworker action                                                                                Packet does / does not tell you\n\n1 Immediate                 Call emergency services for severe breathing difficulty, unresponsiveness,                       Current presentation or immediate safety concern changes the\nemergency                   or another immediate medical emergency. For an immediate threat of                               response regardless of what the packet says. A historical\n                            self-harm or harm to others, use emergency services. Then notify Selma                           behavioral notation alone is not a present emergency.\n                            when safe.\n\n2 Rare exception            Class A TB with a waiver: same-day coordinate with Selma and the                                 Do not impose isolation, cancel ordinary transport\n                            appropriate public-health role. Keep it out of the ordinary appointment                          automatically, notify housing contacts, or make exposure\n                            queue.                                                                                           notices. Do not decide infectiousness.\n\n3 Prioritized               For TB follow-up notations or other findings flagged for follow-up, promptly                     The letter alone is not a severity scale. Use the exact notation,\ncoordination                coordinate with Selma / receiving clinical-public-health role before ordinary                    packet instructions, current facts, and receiving pathway.\n                            screening.\n\n4 Ordinary                  Arrange ordinary domestic screening in 30–90 days after arrival.                                 Screening is a receiving-clinic service, not caseworker\nscheduling                  Higher-priority findings override this ordinary queue.                                           assessment.\n\n Effective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\n briefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                                    2 / 10\n\fOpen the packet without changing it\nThe original record is evidence for receiving professionals. Your work is a narrow, documented handoff.\n\n       Match first\n1      Before acting, verify the packet belongs to the correct person: match name plus date of birth or another packet identifier. Do not copy more identifiers than\n       necessary.\n\n       Preserve the original\n2      Do not alter, annotate over, discard, or separate original overseas medical records. Securely store them and use secure transmission, never personal email,\n       open messaging, or unattended copies.\n\n       Read and relay exactly\n3      Record the classification and annotation exactly as written; attach or securely relay the record when needed. Do not paraphrase it into a diagnosis.\n\n       Exception? Do not reconstruct\n4      Missing, damaged, mismatched, or unreadable packet: secure it and escalate to Selma / the appropriate receiving role. Do not guess or reconstruct\n       contents.\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                            3 / 10\n\fWhat Class A and Class B mean - and do not mean\nThese are federal overseas classifications. Their meaning does not change at the Minnesota-Wisconsin state line.\n\n\nClass A                                                                                                Class B\n\n\nAn overseas immigration medical classification associated with an                                      A significant health condition, finding, or departure from normal health that\ninadmissible condition unless an applicable waiver or resolution permits                               warrants follow-up. It is not itself an inadmissible Class A condition and is not,\ntravel. Arrival may occur with an authorized waiver or other documented                                by itself, a diagnosis of active or contagious disease.\nclearance pathway. It is not a universal statement of present infectiousness.\n\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                               4 / 10\n\f TB notations: quote, route, do not diagnose\n All TB follow-up rows are prioritized coordination ahead of ordinary screening. They are not caseworker symptom screening rules.\n\nExact packet notation                      What it supports                                                                       Caseworker action\n\nClass B0 pulmonary TB                      TB disease was diagnosed overseas and treatment was completed under the                Promptly coordinate domestic follow-up with Selma / receiving\n                                           applicable overseas process before departure.                                          clinical-public-health role; do not recast as untreated active TB.\n\nClass B1 pulmonary TB                      Findings or history require domestic follow-up, without an overseas diagnosis          Prioritized coordination. B1 pulmonary does not mean the person has\n                                           of infectious TB disease at departure.                                                 active TB and does not authorize you to call the person contagious.\n\nClass B1 extrapulmonary TB                 A distinct B1 notation requiring follow-up; it is not synonymous with pulmonary        Prioritized coordination; relay the exact notation.\n                                           infectiousness.\n\nClass B2 TB                                Latent TB infection evaluation - not active TB disease.                                Prioritized coordination; do not diagnose.\n\nClass B3 TB                                Evaluation as a contact of a known TB case; proves neither infection nor               Prioritized coordination; do not make exposure notices.\n                                           disease.\n\n Effective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\n briefing                                                                    Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                                                  5 / 10\n\fNever let a packet delay an emergency\nCurrent symptoms or an immediate safety concern can change the response regardless of what the packet says.\n\n\nRARE, SEPARATE EXCEPTION                                                                    WHAT TO DO\n\nClass A TB with a waiver                                                                    Same-day coordinate with Selma and the appropriate public-health role. Do not put it\nA person may arrive under an authorized waiver or another                                   in the ordinary appointment queue. Do not isolate, cancel ordinary transport\ndocumented clearance pathway. The label alone does not establish                            automatically, warn landlords, or make exposure notifications on your own authority.\npresent infectiousness.\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                        6 / 10\n\fVaccination page: preserve uncertainty\nUse the record to support a receiving-clinic handoff, not to complete an immunization assessment yourself.\n\n\nRead DS-3025 as overseas vaccination documentation\n\n\nIt may show dates or medically relevant reasons a dose was not given (for example, not age appropriate, contraindication, insufficient interval, or unavailable\nvaccine). It is not a complete domestic immunization assessment and does not prove someone is fully vaccinated for every school, employment, or clinical\nstandard.\n\n\nBlank field = unknown or unrecorded, not proof that a vaccine was never given. Do not fill blank fields from memory, family statements, or assumptions.\nPreserve the record and arrange clinical review / immunization assessment when documentation is unclear or incomplete; do not conclude compliance.\n\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                           7 / 10\n\fOne workflow, two state routes\nThe controlled directory supplies the current contacts. Do not invent a deadline, legal mandate, clinic, or public-health contact.\n\n\nSTEP 1 - Select the route                                                                              STEP 2 - Use that receiving route\n\n\nChoose the Minnesota or Wisconsin route by the family’s residence or                                   Minnesota residence / receiving care: route to the Minnesota receiving\nreceiving-care location, not airport pickup location alone. The federal meaning                        clinic and the appropriate Minnesota public-health role.\nof Class A, Class B, B0, B1, B2, and B3 does not change across states.\n                                                                                                       Wisconsin residence / receiving care: route to the Wisconsin receiving\n                                                                                                       clinic and the appropriate Wisconsin public-health role.\n\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                         8 / 10\n\fMake the handoff, not the diagnosis\nThe people arranging housing or transport usually need logistics, not health details.\n\n\nSchedule with the minimum necessary information                                                        Close the loop\n\n\nShare packet diagnoses or classifications only with the receiving clinical /                           Document the appointment or escalation outcome, date/status, destination\npublic-health role that needs them. Do not share them with landlords, housing                          role, and the exact packet label relayed. Do not add a caseworker diagnosis.\nstaff, employers, transport providers, or other people who do not need the                             For a missed, declined, or failed appointment connection: document it,\ninformation. Use a qualified interpreter for health-related communication when                         re-engage respectfully, and escalate to Selma / receiving staff; do not let it\nneeded - not a child or untrained bystander.                                                           disappear.\n\n\n\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                                            9 / 10\n\fPractice: route the record, not the label\nFacilitator prompt: ask for actions, not a diagnosis.\n\nHUDDLE CHECK\n\n\nSituation: A packet for the correctly matched child shows “Class B1 pulmonary TB.” The family will reside in Wisconsin after a Duluth airport pickup. The child\nappears comfortable; there is no immediate safety concern. A housing contact asks whether the child has TB. What are your next three actions?\n\n\n\nDEBRIEF\n\n\n1. Preserve and relay the exact notation securely; do not tell housing the child “has TB.” B1 pulmonary does not mean active TB or authorize calling the child\ncontagious.\n2. Use the Wisconsin residence / receiving-care route, not the Duluth pickup, and prioritize coordination with Selma / Wisconsin receiving clinical-public-health\nrole.\n3. Document the exact label and handoff outcome. Emergency services would come first only if the child had an immediate emergency; the packet alone is not a\ndiagnosis.\n\n\nClinical owner and escalation point: Selma Rutkowski | Effective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal briefing\n\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal\nbriefing                                                                  Stonebridge Board / Tanneryside arrival-support contract                          10 / 10\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}},{"name":"package/private/gold/first_week_action_key.docx","bytes":40768,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/4521ee159cb0ed0ea7d327d3f34ff8e28288e7d881182da50bc8092cd7f1510e.docx","sha256":"4521ee159cb0ed0ea7d327d3f34ff8e28288e7d881182da50bc8092cd7f1510e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"FIRST-WEEK ACTION KEY  Overseas medical packet actions for nonclinical caseworkers\nEffective September 3 | Controlled release: replaces the interim verbal briefing | Clinical owner and escalation point: Selma Rutkowski\n1  IMMEDIATE EMERGENCY\n2  RARE EXCEPTION\n3  PRIORITIZED\n4  ORDINARY\nEmergency services first for severe breathing difficulty, unresponsiveness, another immediate medical emergency, or an immediate threat of self-harm or harm to others. Current presentation changes the response regardless of the packet.\nClass A TB with a waiver: same-day coordinate with Selma and the appropriate public-health role. Keep outside the ordinary appointment queue. Do not decide infectiousness, isolate, cancel ordinary transport automatically, warn housing, or make exposure notices.\nTB follow-up notation or other finding flagged for follow-up: promptly coordinate with Selma / receiving clinical-public-health role before ordinary screening. Letter alone is not priority or severity.\nArrange ordinary domestic screening in 30–90 days after arrival. Higher-priority findings override. A historical behavioral notation alone is not a present emergency.\n\nOPEN • PRESERVE • RELAYCLASS A / B: FAST MEANING\nBefore acting, verify the packet belongs to the correct person: match name plus date of birth or another packet identifier. Copy no more identifiers than needed.\nDo not alter, annotate over, discard, or separate original overseas medical records. Securely store and transmit; never use personal email, open messaging, or unattended copies.\nRecord and relay the exact classification / annotation (secure record attached when appropriate). Do not paraphrase into a diagnosis. Missing, damaged, mismatched, or unreadable? Secure it and escalate to Selma / appropriate receiving role; do not guess or reconstruct.\nClass A: overseas immigration medical classification associated with an inadmissible condition unless an applicable waiver or resolution permits travel. Arrival can occur through an authorized waiver or other documented clearance pathway; it is not a universal statement of present infectiousness.\nClass B: significant condition, finding, or departure from normal health warranting follow-up; not itself an inadmissible Class A condition and not, by itself, active or contagious disease.\nMental-health or behavioral Class A/B notations need careful clinical follow-up; they do not by themselves establish current dangerousness.\nTB: READ EXACTLY, THEN ROUTEVACCINATION DOCUMENTATION\nClass B0 pulmonary TB: TB disease diagnosed overseas; treatment completed under the applicable overseas process before departure. Prioritized follow-up; do not recast as untreated active TB.\nClass B1 pulmonary TB: findings/history require domestic follow-up without an overseas diagnosis of infectious TB disease at departure. Prioritized follow-up. Does not mean active TB or authorize calling the person contagious.\nClass B1 extrapulmonary TB: distinct notation requiring follow-up; not synonymous with pulmonary infectiousness. Prioritized follow-up.\nClass B2 TB: latent TB infection evaluation, not active TB disease. Prioritized follow-up.\nClass B3 TB: evaluation as contact of a known TB case; proves neither infection nor disease. Prioritized follow-up; no caseworker exposure notices.\nDS-3025 is overseas vaccination documentation, not a complete domestic immunization assessment. A blank field does not prove a vaccine was never given. Entries may be dates or medically relevant reasons no dose was given (for example, not age appropriate, contraindication, insufficient interval, unavailable vaccine).\nDo not fill blanks from memory, family statements, or assumptions. Unclear/incomplete documentation: arrange clinical review or immunization assessment, not a caseworker compliance conclusion.\nSELECT THE STATE ROUTEMINIMUM-NECESSARY HANDOFFCLOSE THE LOOP\nChoose the Minnesota or Wisconsin route by the family’s residence or receiving-care location, not airport pickup location alone.\nMINNESOTA residence / receiving care: route to the Minnesota receiving clinic and appropriate Minnesota public-health role.\nWISCONSIN residence / receiving care: route to the Wisconsin receiving clinic and appropriate Wisconsin public-health role.\nFederal overseas classification meaning does not change across states. Use the controlled directory for contacts; do not invent deadlines, legal mandates, or contacts. Uncertain, conflicting, or cross-border? Escalate to Selma; do not guess.\nShare classifications only with the receiving clinical/public-health role that needs them. Do not share packet diagnoses or classifications with landlords, housing staff, employers, transport providers, or other people who do not need the information.\nUse a qualified interpreter for health-related communication when needed - not a child or untrained bystander.\nSay: “The overseas record contains a Class B1 pulmonary TB classification requiring follow-up. I can send the record securely to the receiving role.”\nDocument date/status, destination role, appointment or escalation outcome, and exact packet label relayed - never a caseworker diagnosis. Missed, declined, or failed connection: document, re-engage respectfully, and escalate to Selma / receiving staff.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"`caseworker_training_deck.pdf` is present and is a readable PDF.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted deck file and PDF metadata.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, PDF format, and that all pages open successfully."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"`first_week_action_key.docx` is present and is a readable Word document.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted action-key file and DOCX package.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, DOCX format, and that the document opens successfully."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered training deck contains no more than 10 slides/pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"PDF page count.","grader_hint":"Use the PDF page count; the maximum is 10 pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered action key occupies exactly one page without clipped or hidden content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX pages.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX and confirm a single page with no text outside margins, cropped tables, or overflow."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Both files remain legible at normal viewing or print size and use visual hierarchy that supports rapid scanning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered deck and action key.","grader_hint":"Review typography, contrast, spacing, and information density; credit only if neither file requires zooming or deciphering dense prose."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package is written for nonclinical caseworkers rather than clinicians, lawyers, or patients.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Language, examples, and instructions throughout both files.","grader_hint":"Look for plain operational language and explanations of unavoidable clinical terminology."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck explicitly limits the caseworker role to observing, recording, scheduling, safeguarding information, and escalating rather than diagnosing or treating.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope statement in the deck.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording only if both permitted actions and the diagnostic/treatment boundary are clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package deliberately adopts either a common workflow with state branches or visibly separate Minnesota and Wisconsin lanes, and the chosen structure is unmistakable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional organization in both deliverables.","grader_hint":"Either defensible design earns full credit; do not require one particular model."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck has a coherent instructional progression that can be delivered in a 20-minute huddle by a nonclinical supervisor.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sequence and density of the deck.","grader_hint":"Credit a progression from purpose and boundaries through packet interpretation to action and practice; reject a glossary-only information dump."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck gives the learner at least one opportunity to apply the guidance to a synthetic packet scenario.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario, knowledge check, or facilitated exercise in the deck.","grader_hint":"The exercise must ask for a scheduling, escalation, privacy, or communication decision rather than clinical diagnosis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any learner exercise includes an answer or debrief that explains the safe action and the limit of the packet evidence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Answer or facilitator debrief in the deck.","grader_hint":"Confirm the learner can verify the reasoning without consulting the author."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials direct staff to verify that the packet belongs to the correct person before acting on it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Packet-opening guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for a minimum identity match such as name plus date of birth or another packet identifier, without requiring unnecessary copying."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials require classifications and annotations to be transcribed or relayed exactly rather than paraphrased into a diagnosis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Documentation or escalation guidance.","grader_hint":"Credit wording such as quote the label exactly, attach the secure record, or do not translate B1 into active TB."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package tells staff not to alter, annotate over, discard, or separate original overseas medical records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Record-handling instruction.","grader_hint":"Equivalent preservation language is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package provides a safe route for a missing, damaged, mismatched, or unreadable packet without asking the caseworker to reconstruct its contents.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception handling in deck or key.","grader_hint":"Look for secure escalation to Selma or the appropriate receiving role and an instruction not to guess."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class A is accurately described as an overseas immigration medical classification associated with an inadmissible condition unless an applicable waiver or resolution permits travel, not as a universal statement of present infectiousness.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class A explanation.","grader_hint":"The wording may be simplified but must preserve both immigration significance and the limit on what can be inferred clinically."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials explain that a person may arrive with a Class A notation under an authorized waiver or other documented clearance pathway.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class A or rare-exception guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit reason why arrival with a Class A label is possible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B is accurately described as a significant health condition, finding, or departure from normal health that warrants follow-up but is not itself an inadmissible Class A condition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class B explanation.","grader_hint":"Accept concise wording that accurately separates Class B significance from Class A inadmissibility."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package explicitly states that a Class B notation is not, by itself, a diagnosis of active or contagious disease.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class B boundary statement.","grader_hint":"This distinction must be direct enough to prevent a caseworker from relabeling the person."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Class A and B explanations avoid presenting the letter alone as a complete scheduling priority or clinical severity scale.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class overview and action logic.","grader_hint":"Credit if the specific condition, notation, waiver status, current presentation, and receiving pathway determine action rather than the letter alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B0 pulmonary TB is accurately distinguished as TB disease diagnosed overseas with treatment completed under the applicable overseas process before departure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB classification content.","grader_hint":"The material must not merge B0 into B1 or describe it as untreated active TB."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B1 pulmonary TB is accurately tied to findings or history requiring domestic follow-up without an overseas diagnosis of infectious TB disease at departure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB classification content.","grader_hint":"Accept simplified language that preserves the need for follow-up and the absence of a packet-supported active infectious TB diagnosis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials directly state that B1 pulmonary does not mean the person has active TB and does not authorize the caseworker to call the person contagious.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"B1 pulmonary explanation or communication warning.","grader_hint":"Both the diagnosis and contagiousness limits must be apparent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B1 extrapulmonary TB is identified as a distinct notation requiring follow-up rather than being treated as synonymous with pulmonary infectiousness.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB classification content.","grader_hint":"Look for a distinct description or action row for extrapulmonary B1."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B2 TB is accurately associated with latent TB infection evaluation rather than active TB disease.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB classification content.","grader_hint":"The term latent TB infection or an accurate plain-language equivalent should appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class B3 TB is accurately associated with evaluation as a contact of a known TB case rather than proof of infection or disease.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB classification content.","grader_hint":"Confirm the contact status and the limit on inference are both conveyed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The TB classifications are translated into differentiated caseworker actions rather than presented only as definitions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"TB action table, flow, or state lanes.","grader_hint":"The learner should be able to distinguish routine scheduling, prioritized clinical coordination, rare escalation, and emergency response without diagnosing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Class A TB with a waiver is presented as a rare, separate exception that must not enter the ordinary appointment queue.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rare-exception guidance in deck and/or key.","grader_hint":"The exception must be visually or verbally separate from general TB guidance and have a distinct action."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Class A TB waiver pathway directs prompt same-day coordination with Selma and the appropriate public-health role while preserving emergency escalation for an immediate emergency.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rare-exception action.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent same-business-day language. The instruction must not tell the caseworker to make an infectiousness determination."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rare Class A TB guidance does not instruct caseworkers to impose isolation, cancel ordinary transport automatically, notify housing contacts, or make exposure notifications on their own authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Class A TB action language.","grader_hint":"Award credit when the pathway clearly places clinical and public-health decisions with authorized professionals."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package makes clear that current symptoms or an immediate safety concern can change the response regardless of what the packet says.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Emergency boundary statement.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit packet-versus-current-condition distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The emergency guidance directs staff to emergency services for severe breathing difficulty, unresponsiveness, or another immediate medical emergency.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Emergency examples and action.","grader_hint":"The examples need not be exhaustive, but the response must be immediate and unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The emergency guidance separately addresses an immediate threat of self-harm or harm to others without treating a historical behavioral notation as a present emergency.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Behavioral-health emergency guidance.","grader_hint":"Confirm that current observed risk, not the packet label alone, triggers emergency action."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The materials do not turn caseworkers into symptom screeners or provide a clinical decision rule that exceeds their role.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All symptom and escalation content.","grader_hint":"Plain recognition of obvious emergencies is acceptable; diagnostic checklists, medical clearance, and clinical triage instructions are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The vaccination record is identified as the overseas vaccination documentation, such as DS-3025, without presenting it as a complete domestic immunization assessment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vaccination content.","grader_hint":"Look for the form name or an accurate description plus the limitation on completeness."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package explicitly states that a blank vaccination field does not prove that a vaccine was never given.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Blank-field guidance.","grader_hint":"The limit must be direct and understandable to a day-one caseworker."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package tells caseworkers not to fill blank vaccination fields from memory, family statements, or assumptions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vaccination documentation instruction.","grader_hint":"Credit any explicit instruction to leave interpretation and record completion to qualified clinical staff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The vaccination explanation recognizes that entries may document dates or medically relevant reasons a dose was not given, rather than treating every non-date entry as refusal.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vaccination legend or explanation.","grader_hint":"Accept examples such as not age appropriate, contraindication, insufficient interval, or unavailable vaccine; a complete list is not required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Unclear or incomplete vaccination documentation leads to clinical review or immunization assessment, not a caseworker conclusion about compliance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vaccination action pathway.","grader_hint":"The receiving clinic or Selma may be the review destination."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The materials avoid promising that an overseas vaccination page proves a person is fully vaccinated under every domestic school, employment, or clinical standard.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vaccination claims throughout both files.","grader_hint":"Award credit when domestic assessment is left to the appropriate receiving authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials explain that mental-health or behavioral Class A/B notations require careful clinical follow-up and do not by themselves establish current dangerousness.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Non-TB classification or communication content.","grader_hint":"Look for both follow-up significance and the prohibition on inferring present risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"References to sexually transmitted infections, mental health, disability, or other sensitive conditions are neutral, specific, and non-stigmatizing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology and examples throughout the package.","grader_hint":"Reject moralizing, alarmist, or identity-defining language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package prohibits sharing packet diagnoses or classifications with landlords, housing staff, employers, transport providers, or other parties who do not need the information.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy and communication guidance.","grader_hint":"The instruction may use a minimum-necessary or need-to-know formulation but must cover nonclinical housing contacts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package requires secure storage and secure transmission of packet information rather than personal email, open messaging, or unattended copies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Record-handling and referral guidance.","grader_hint":"A stable role-based instruction is sufficient; no application-specific click path is needed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials use a minimum-necessary approach when communicating for appointment scheduling.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scheduling call guidance or communication script.","grader_hint":"The caseworker should relay only what the receiving role needs and avoid narrating the entire packet."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package provides at least one usable model phrase that accurately describes a packet notation without converting it into a diagnosis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Communication example or script.","grader_hint":"For example, a phrase may state that the overseas record contains a B1 pulmonary classification requiring follow-up; exact wording is not prescribed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package directs staff to use a qualified interpreter for health-related communication when needed rather than relying on a child or untrained bystander.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Communication or appointment guidance.","grader_hint":"Both the positive interpreter direction and avoidance of children or bystanders should be evident."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The materials distinguish the stable federal meaning of overseas classifications from state- or locality-specific routing after arrival.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction comparison.","grader_hint":"The package should not imply that B1, B2, B3, or Class A changes definition when the state changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Minnesota is named and given an identifiable operational route based on the family’s Minnesota residence and the appropriate receiving clinical or public-health role.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Minnesota branch or lane.","grader_hint":"Role-based routing is acceptable; an unverified agency name or number is not required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Wisconsin is named and given an identifiable operational route based on the family’s Wisconsin residence and the appropriate receiving clinical or public-health role.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Wisconsin branch or lane.","grader_hint":"Role-based routing is acceptable; an unverified agency name or number is not required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The jurisdiction guidance uses residence or receiving-care location rather than airport pickup location alone to select the operational route.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"State-selection instruction.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear rule that prevents a Duluth or Superior pickup from automatically determining the wrong state lane."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The jurisdiction comparison avoids unsupported state legal mandates, fabricated deadlines, and unapproved clinic or public-health contact information.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All Minnesota and Wisconsin claims.","grader_hint":"Stable role-based differences and a direction to use the controlled directory are appropriate; specific unsupported legal assertions are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"An uncertain, conflicting, or cross-border routing situation is directed to Selma rather than resolved by caseworker guesswork.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional exception handling.","grader_hint":"The designated clinical escalation role or Selma by name must be identifiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package gives a defensible ordinary domestic screening target, expressed as a 30–90 day post-arrival window, while making clear that higher-priority findings may require earlier coordination.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"General scheduling guidance.","grader_hint":"Accept typographic variants such as 30-90 days. The window must not be presented as a safe waiting period for every classification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package explicitly identifies where the general appointment rule of thumb stops applying, including prioritized TB follow-up, a Class A TB waiver, and an immediate emergency.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scheduling hierarchy or action flow.","grader_hint":"All three boundaries must be visible; they may be distributed across the deck and key."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The action logic places emergency response ahead of packet processing, the Class A TB waiver pathway outside routine scheduling, prioritized clinical/public-health coordination ahead of ordinary screening, and ordinary screening last.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ordering or branching in the action key and supporting deck content.","grader_hint":"Assess the operational order, not the exact graphic format."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package tells staff to document the appointment or escalation outcome and the exact packet label relayed, without adding a caseworker diagnosis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Close-the-loop guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for a record of date/status, destination or role, and exact notation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A missed, declined, or failed appointment connection has a defined follow-up route rather than disappearing from the workflow.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scheduling exception guidance.","grader_hint":"Credit instructions to record the outcome and re-engage or escalate to Selma/receiving staff without coercive language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The one-page key functions as a true point-of-use action aid, with quickly distinguishable actions for emergency, rare exception, prioritized follow-up, routine scheduling, and uncertainty.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered action key.","grader_hint":"A new caseworker should be able to locate the applicable action in seconds without reading the page linearly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The action key is usable on its own without requiring definitions or essential instructions available only in the deck.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action key considered independently.","grader_hint":"Check whether a day-one user can interpret the listed labels, select a jurisdiction, preserve privacy, and choose an action without asking the author."},{"rubric_item_id":"R61","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every classification meaning stated in both files is substantively consistent between the deck and the action key.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-file comparison of Class A/B and TB classification statements.","grader_hint":"Compare shared labels line by line; condensed wording is acceptable if it preserves the same meaning and limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R62","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every scheduling window or escalation timing stated in both files agrees exactly.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-file comparison of all numerical and verbal timeframes.","grader_hint":"Compare figures such as 30–90 days and terms such as same day; formatting variants are acceptable, contradictory timing is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R63","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Minnesota and Wisconsin state-selection rule is consistent between the deck and the action key.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-file comparison of jurisdiction routing.","grader_hint":"Both files must direct the same family to the same state lane under the same facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R64","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both files identify September 3 as the effective date or otherwise clearly tie the package to the September 3 release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Effective-date statements in both deliverables.","grader_hint":"The date may appear in a footer, title area, or revision statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R65","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package states that it replaces the interim verbal briefing and identifies Selma as the clinical owner or escalation point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Revision and ownership statement.","grader_hint":"The statement may be concise but must make the controlled replacement and owner identifiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R66","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Taken together, the package gives a day-one caseworker enough specific, safe guidance to act without contacting the author for routine interpretation while preserving escalation for genuine uncertainty.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Holistic review of both deliverables.","grader_hint":"Award full credit only if the materials are operationally complete, clinically restrained, non-stigmatizing, privacy-conscious, and practical under first-week conditions."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A refugee health nurse practitioner is replacing inconsistent verbal guidance used by nonclinical resettlement caseworkers in Minnesota and Wisconsin. The package must translate overseas medical classifications into safe scheduling and escalation actions without diagnosing, stigmatizing, or disclosing unnecessary health information.","difficulty_sources":["Translating clinically meaningful immigration medical classifications into actions safe for nonclinical staff","Bounding a familiar routine-scheduling shortcut without making the general workflow unusable","Separating a rare Class A TB waiver pathway from routine guidance without creating stigma or unsupported infectiousness claims","Designing a defensible two-jurisdiction workflow without current local contact details","Maintaining exact consistency between a teaching deck and a standalone one-page action key","Minimizing sensitive health information while preserving enough detail for scheduling and escalation"],"authoring_plan":{"solver_instance":"A refugee-health nurse practitioner creates a new instructional package for nonclinical resettlement caseworkers who inspect overseas medical packets and arrange first-week care in Minnesota and Wisconsin.","intended_verdict":"A passing solution safely converts overseas medical labels into operational scheduling and escalation choices, distinguishes what the record supports from what it does not, treats the rare Class A TB waiver pathway separately, and remains usable under the deck and one-page limits.","reference_materials":"No reference files are built or supplied. The gold relies on stable occupational knowledge about overseas medical examination classifications, domestic refugee health screening, privacy, interpreter use, emergency boundaries, and nonclinical scope.","professional_tensions":["The commonly used ordinary-appointment shortcut must be bounded: routine domestic screening may use a 30–90 day post-arrival window, but prioritized TB follow-up, a Class A TB waiver, and an immediate emergency cannot be placed into that default queue.","Class A TB with a waiver is rare but severe enough to require a visibly separate same-day clinical and public-health coordination route; the route must not authorize caseworker isolation, exposure notification, transport cancellation, or a conclusion about current infectiousness.","The package must permit confident scheduling while preventing diagnosis, triage beyond obvious emergency recognition, and unsupported disclosure.","The jurisdiction design is deliberately ambiguous. A common workflow with Minnesota and Wisconsin branches and separate state lanes are both acceptable if state selection is explicit and consistent.","Federal overseas classification meanings remain constant across states, while receiving-care and public-health routing may depend on the family's residence and local pathway.","The unsettled contact directory means the gold must use stable roles and Selma as the designated escalation point rather than fabricated contacts."],"gold_relationships":["The general screening window is 30–90 days after arrival, but any higher-priority classification or current concern overrides the ordinary queue.","Emergency response precedes packet processing; a Class A TB waiver follows a distinct same-day coordination path; prioritized findings are coordinated before ordinary screening.","Class A describes an immigration medical classification with inadmissibility significance unless resolved or waived; it does not universally prove present infectiousness.","Class B describes a significant condition or finding warranting follow-up but is not itself an inadmissible Class A condition or proof of active contagious disease.","B0 pulmonary TB reflects overseas-diagnosed TB disease with treatment completed before departure under the applicable process.","B1 pulmonary indicates findings or history requiring follow-up without a packet-supported diagnosis of infectious TB disease at departure.","B1 extrapulmonary is distinct from pulmonary infectiousness; B2 concerns latent TB infection evaluation; B3 concerns contact evaluation and proves neither infection nor disease.","A blank vaccination field is unknown or unrecorded, not proof that no vaccine occurred; caseworkers preserve it and refer it for clinical assessment.","State routing is selected using residence or receiving-care context, not airport pickup location alone.","All shared classifications, time windows, effective dates, and state-selection rules must be consistent across both deliverables."],"required_vocabulary_and_local_conventions":{"filenames":["caseworker_training_deck.pdf","first_week_action_key.docx"],"jurisdictions":["Minnesota","Wisconsin"],"effective_date":"September 3","clinical_escalation_owner":"Selma Rutkowski","controlled_change":"The package replaces the interim verbal briefing.","classification_terms":["Class A","Class B","Class A TB with waiver","Class B0 pulmonary TB","Class B1 pulmonary TB","Class B1 extrapulmonary TB","Class B2 TB","Class B3 TB","DS-3025"]},"imperfections_the_solution_must_handle":["Caseworkers may equate any B notation with routine priority.","Caseworkers may repeat B1 pulmonary as active or contagious TB.","Blank vaccination fields may be misread as proof that no vaccination occurred.","A rare Class A TB waiver arrival can be lost in a routine queue.","Pickup location, residence, receiving clinic, and public-health jurisdiction may differ.","The local contact directory is unavailable, requiring role-based routing.","Sensitive classifications may be disclosed to housing or transport contacts without a minimum-necessary boundary.","A packet label may distract staff from an immediate condition occurring in front of them."],"space_feasibility":"Ten deck pages can support a concise role boundary, packet workflow, Class A/B overview, TB distinctions, vaccination interpretation, privacy and communication guidance, state routing, emergency and rare-exception paths, and a short applied scenario. A one-page key can contain a compact priority ladder, classification-to-action rows, state selector, privacy reminders, and escalation information without reproducing the teaching detail.","gold_construction_notes":"Create a polished reference deck and key using synthetic examples only. Verify all TB and vaccination language for factual restraint. Render both outputs offline, inspect the deck page count and action-key pagination, then audit every shared label, timeframe, date, and jurisdiction rule for agreement. Walk each rubric item against the rendered artifacts before approval."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"training_deck","filename":"caseworker_training_deck.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A caseworker-facing training deck for a 20-minute staff huddle on using overseas medical packet information during the first arrival week.","required":true,"constraints":["No more than 10 slides/pages; suitable for facilitation by a nonclinical supervisor; effective for Minnesota and Wisconsin operations; must agree with the action key on shared labels, figures, and timing."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"action_key","filename":"first_week_action_key.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A one-page standalone action key for a day-one caseworker using an overseas medical packet while arranging first-week services.","required":true,"constraints":["Exactly one rendered page; usable without the deck; must agree with the deck on shared labels, figures, and timing."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"clinical_administrative_package","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":7.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be authored by a refugee-health clinician familiar with overseas medical examination classifications and independently reviewed by a second clinician or public-health practitioner for classification accuracy, scope boundaries, privacy, and usability by nonclinical resettlement staff. A document-production review should separately confirm rendering, page limits, and cross-file consistency.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Exact Minnesota and Wisconsin referral contacts and locally mandated reporting channels are intentionally excluded because no controlled directory or current local source is supplied. The gold therefore uses stable role-based routing and does not score unverified agency names, telephone numbers, or state-specific legal deadlines."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T00:53:13+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T00:53:13+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T00:53:13+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T00:53:13+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T00:53:13+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c3/runs/3ac2c00febcf3308","declared_deliverables":["caseworker_training_deck.pdf","first_week_action_key.docx"]};
